+# xmake: our fork
+
+## What this is
+
+Our fork of [xmake](https://github.com/xmake-io/xmake), `github.com/kern0x1b/xmake`. The working
+branch `charon` starts at tag `v3.1.1`, the release the machine runs (Homebrew `xmake 3.1.1`), and
+carries only our fixes on top, so the machine gets the same xmake plus them. `dev` is upstream's
+default branch, left as forked. Nothing goes to `xmake-io` as a pull request (workspace
+`AGENTS.md` §4); `upstream` is fetch-only.
+
+What our commits change and why is in their messages: `git log v3.1.1..charon`. Upstream's own
+documents: `README.md`, `CONTRIBUTING.md`, <https://xmake.io>.
+
+## Layout
+
+- `core/src/` — the C core (`xmake/`, `cli/`) and its dependencies. `tbox`, `luajit`, `lua`,
+  `lua-cjson`, `lz4`, `sv` and `pdcurses` are git submodules.
+- `xmake/` — the Lua scripts the binary runs (`core/`, `modules/`, `actions/`, `plugins/`, `rules/`,
+  ...). Installed as `<prefix>/share/xmake`; almost every fix lands here.
+- `tests/` — xmake's own test system: `tests/run.lua <filter>` runs every `test.lua` under the
+  matching directory, each `test_*` function with the helpers in `tests/test_utils/`.
+- Addons, where our fixes are so far:
+  - `xmake/core/package/addon.lua` — the registry `~/.xmake/addons/addons.conf`, the `active`
+    version of each addon, the per-process pins;
+  - `xmake/core/project/addons.lua` and `xmake/core/project/project.lua` — a project's
+    `xmake-addons.lock`, its auto-fetch and pinning;
+  - `xmake/modules/private/action/addon/impl/install_addons.lua` — the auto-fetch installer, it
+    writes the lock;
+  - `xmake/modules/private/action/require/impl/actions/install.lua` (`_register_addon`) — the
+    registration after an install;
+  - `tests/actions/addon/` — their tests, fixtures and fixture projects.
+- Generated, never source: `build/` and `makefile` (from `./configure`).
+
+## Build and check
+
+Clone with `--recurse-submodules`; after checking out another tag or branch run
+`git submodule update --init --recursive` (tag `v3.1.1` pins another `tbox` than `dev`).
+
+Build and install into our own prefix, never over Homebrew, and only through the slots:
+
+```
+$HOME/Git/projects/ios/coordination/heavy.sh sh -c "./configure --prefix=$HOME/.local/xmake-kern && make -j8 && make install"
+$HOME/.local/xmake-kern/bin/xmake --version     # xmake v3.1.1+charon.<commit>
+```
+
+The default `./configure` builds the Lua runtime, like Homebrew's. The fleet runs this binary only
+after the owner switches to it.
+
+Tests. They install and remove addons and register repositories in the global directory, so
+always give them their own:
+
+```
+export XMAKE_GLOBALDIR=$PWD/.agent-work/runs/<task>/global
+XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR=$PWD/xmake xmake lua -v tests/run.lua actions/addon
+```
+
+- `XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR=$PWD/xmake` runs the scripts of this tree with the installed 3.1.1 binary;
+  the C core of `charon` is that of `v3.1.1`, so no build is needed for a Lua change.
+- The control, without our fixes: `XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR=/opt/homebrew/Cellar/xmake/3.1.1/share/xmake`
+  (the same scripts as tag `v3.1.1`), or a detached worktree at an earlier commit.
+- The built binary: `env -u XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR PATH=$HOME/.local/xmake-kern/bin:$PATH xmake lua -v
+  tests/run.lua actions/addon`. The tests run `xmake` from `PATH`, so set it for that command only.
+- The first run clones xmake-repo into the new global directory (network, about 30 MB).
+
+## Conventions
+
+- Work goes on `charon`, forward only. Upstream fixes come in from `upstream` only when the owner
+  decides; moving `charon` to a newer upstream release is the owner's call.
+- Commits: an imperative subject, no prefix; the body says the cause, the change, and the test and
+  its control. Trailer `Co-Authored-By`.
+- Upstream's code style: four spaces, `-- @note` / `-- @see` comments above the code, the helpers
+  of `core.base.*` rather than new ones.
+- Every fix has a test in `tests/`, and the test is shown to fail without the fix (the control
+  above) and pass with it.
+- Push only `charon` to `origin`, only after the owner's word and the reviewer's verdict. The
+  commit hooks are `$HOME/Git/projects/ios/coordination/hooks`; never `--no-verify`.
+
+## Traps
+
+- A test run without `XMAKE_GLOBALDIR` writes the shared `~/.xmake` (`addons.conf`, the repository
+  cache) → always set it; the shared store is every band's.
+- Never `xrepo`/`xmake require --force` against the shared store and never edit
+  `~/.xmake/addons/addons.conf` by hand → the fleet builds from them; that needs the coordinator.
+- `XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR` left in the environment overrides the installed binary's own scripts →
+  `env -u XMAKE_PROGRAM_DIR` when checking the built binary.
+- In the sandbox `semver.select` raises when nothing matches and `semver.satisfies` raises on a
+  range that does not parse, e.g. `latest` → a caller that expects no match wraps them in `try`.
+
+## Workspace
+
+Workspace procedures, by path: `$HOME/Git/projects/ios/.agents/skills/self-review/SKILL.md`
+(before every hand-over), `.../session-handoff/SKILL.md` (at 200k context or at the end),
+`.../worktree-sweep/SKILL.md`. Heavy jobs: `$HOME/Git/projects/ios/coordination/heavy.sh`.
+The charon gate that checks this binary: `$HOME/Git/projects/ios/coordination/build-gate.lua`,
+run as in charon's skill `backports-gate`, with the fork's binary in place of `xmake`.
